jules-s: heads up : we're getting a few bug reports where the SDK breaks in Next.js edge deployments because users have accidentally polyfilled fetch in their build. discussion: should SDK prefer global fetch, lazy-load undici in Node, or ship a tiny bundled polyfill?

harper-ops: strong +1 to NOT bundling any polyfills. Edge builds must stay tiny. prefer globalThis.fetch when available, and lazy import undici only in Node runtimes.

mina-rt: agree. small nuance: some consumers use require() (CJS) and expect synchronous module init. we should provide a synchronous getter that returns the global fetch or null and an async loader that does dynamic import('undici') only if needed. sample API: getFetchSync(), loadFetch() -> Promise<Fetch>.

sam-eng: sketch I threw together:

```js
// fetch-select.mjs
export function getFetchSync() {
  return typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function' ? globalThis.fetch : null;
}

export async function loadFetch() {
  const f = getFetchSync();
  if (f) return f;
  // dynamic import so bundlers exclude undici from edge bundles
  const mod = await import('undici');
  return mod.fetch;
}
```

harper-ops: perfect. For CJS consumers we can provide a tiny require shim that exports the same two functions (loadFetch returns a Promise). keeps require() semantics intact and avoids top-level await.

mina-rt: typings: we should not force consumers to enable lib: ["DOM"] in tsconfig. my proposal: ship our own minimal types in types/fetch-lite.d.ts and re-export a `type FetchLike = (input: any, init?: any) => Promise<any>` in our public d.ts. also add a docs note: if you want full DOM types add "DOM" to tsconfig.

jules-s: +1 to minimal types. alternative is using conditional types with import('undici').RequestInit but that still forces consumers to install undici types. keeping minimal in-package types is safest.

pkg-bot: packaging note: optionalDependency vs optionalPeerDependency decision: optionalDependency will be installed by npm by default which can be noisy for edge users. optionalPeerDependency will show warnings unless the consumer installs it. we could mark undici as optionalPeerDependency and add clear docs, or leave it out and document `npm i undici` for Node users.

harper-ops: I prefer leaving undici out of dependencies entirely and documenting the install step for Node <20. then set up CI to test with undici installed and also with raw Node fetch. simpler semantics and no surprise installs.

mina-rt: CI matrix: run tests under Node 16/18/20 (with/without undici), Bun, and a Workers runtime (miniflare) to cover fetch differences (streams vs body types). add a gating job to catch regressions.

sam-eng: retry and auth behavior: implement retries above the fetch layer so token refresh and idempotent retries are consistent across runtimes. do not rely on undici's agent for retry semantics by default. expose extra config to opt in to connection pooling tuning if user provides undici.

jules-s: docs TODOs: 1) README section "fetch/runtime compatibility" with code snippets for Next.js (edge vs server), Vite, webpack (how not to polyfill), and pnpm tips. 2) TS FAQ with copy-paste fix (either add DOM to tsconfig or import our fetch-lite types). 3) packaging note about why undici is not bundled.

harper-ops: I'll add the webpack/Vite snippets and a note about pnpm hoisting that can prevent optional deps from being resolved the same way as npm.

mina-rt: action items: sam -> implement the adapter + CJS shim; mina -> add fetch-lite.d.ts and update exported types; harper -> docs and packaging guidance; jules -> add CI matrix job. I'll open a PR template for reviewers to validate edge bundle size before merging.

sam-eng: thanks all, I'll start the PR and include the loadFetch/getFetchSync utilities + tests.
